Roast.pet Weird Builder Projects =E2=80=94 4 useful projects

Screened 25+ plausible projects across GitHub and web results covering
pet/animal landmarks, lip sync, browser video rendering, captions,
WebAR, matting, Etsy/POD and mockup automation. Deduplicated against
prior Roast.pet scanner finds. Four new projects are worth retaining.

1) dougalder/lip-sync-generator =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW / COPY UI MECHANISMS

URL: https://github.com/dougalder/lip-sync-generator

Creator: Doug Alder

Activity: 70 commits; repository crawled current on 2026-09-20

License: Apache-2.0; embedded Rhubarb WASM is MIT, CMU Sphinx model
BSD, fonts OFL

What it solves: This is much more complete than merely calling
Rhubarb. One offline HTML file takes speech, derives viseme timing,
exposes an editable exposure sheet, lets the operator trim/split
clips, place the mouth over a still/video puppet, keyframe the mouth
position, blur/cover an existing printed mouth, queue up to 24 clips,
and export chart/timing, transparent GIF/APNG, sprite sheet, PNG
sequence, chroma MP4 or composited video. It includes 15 mouth styles
in front/3-quarter/profile angles and accepts custom mouth art.

Install/demo: download lip-sync-generator.html and open it directly;
no server/account/build/network required. A served version can preload
custom lips/ sets.

Dependencies/hardware: ordinary browser; Rhubarb/PocketSphinx WASM; CPU onl=
y.

Evidence: 70 commits, manual + offline test that actively refuses
network access, multiple export paths, editable exposure-sheet
workflow.

Work replaced: arbitrary-audio lip-sync UI, mouth placement editor,
manual correction UI, sprite/timing export, potentially 1k+ lines of
bespoke tooling.

Integration: LOW.

Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM.

Exact experiment: load one canonical Roast.pet transparent pet and our
five mouth sprites, run 20 arbitrary audio clips, manually correct any
bad visemes, export timing JSON + transparent frames, and measure
median operator correction time. Compare against raw Rhubarb WASM.

Verdict: REUSE NOW; copy the correction/exposure-sheet/placement
mechanisms even if HeadTTS remains the normal generated-speech path.

2) IgorShadurin/yumcut-video-ads =E2=80=94 BENCHMARK NOW / REUSE RENDERER

URL: https://github.com/IgorShadurin/yumcut-video-ads

Creator: Igor Shadurin

Activity: 5 commits; current repository

License: MIT; distributed Mediabunny dependency MPL-2.0

What it solves: A tiny one-star browser-native production renderer
using WebCodecs + worker WebGL2. It composes ordered video/image/audio
layers, trims/loops/fades/transforms/crops them, mixes audio, and
exports MP4 H.264/AAC or WebM VP9/Opus without a render server. It has
support analysis before rendering, hardware acceleration requests,
bounded decode/encode queues, progress/cancellation, Cache
Storage/OPFS support, and 360p=E2=80=934K / up-to-five-minute qualification
targets.

Install/API: npm install github:IgorShadurin/yumcut-video-ads;
runnable Next.js, React/Vite and vanilla TypeScript demos are
included.

Dependencies/hardware: modern secure-context browser with WebCodecs;
WebGL2 with Canvas2D fallback. Desktop Chromium is the primary target;
mobile depends on actual codec support.

Evidence: browser tests, demo production builds, benchmark records,
deterministic media fixtures and explicit detectSupport()/analyze()
paths.

Work replaced: our browser compositor, muxing/encoding plumbing, media
support detection, cache/progress/cancellation logic =E2=80=94 potentially
several thousand lines.

Integration: MEDIUM.

Maintenance risk: MEDIUM because WebCodecs support varies by browser.

Exact experiment: encode the canonical 15-second Roast.pet vertical
roast entirely client-side: podium background +
transparent/stacked-alpha pet layer + captions + TTS + music sting.
Benchmark iPhone Safari, Android Chrome and desktop Chrome for render
time, memory, output size and visual parity. If mobile support is
adequate, avoid building a server renderer for
interactive/order-preview work.

Verdict: BENCHMARK NOW / REUSE RENDERER.

3) Fats403/remotion-captions-kit =E2=80=94 REUSE NOW if Remotion stays in t=
he stack

URL: https://github.com/Fats403/remotion-captions-kit

Creator: Fats403 / videoventure.ai

Activity: 5 commits; current repository

License: MIT

What it solves: The annoying layer after word timestamps. It adds
human-like caption pagination over @remotion/captions: sentence
boundaries, silence gaps, max-duration and max-character caps,
clause-aware breaks, orphan balancing, flash-page merging,
abbreviation handling and malformed-input cleanup. It also ships six
animated presets including karaoke, kinetic slam, pill karaoke and
editorial emphasis. Placement is normalized by composition dimensions,
so the same theme works at different resolutions.

Install: npm i remotion-captions-kit; peer deps are Remotion v4, React
and @remotion/captions.

Dependencies/hardware: normal Remotion render stack.

Evidence: explicit Vitest suite for pagination/SRT/emphasis plus demo
studio; code addresses known @remotion/captions whitespace/timing
footguns.

Work replaced: custom short-form caption paginator, word activation
timing and most caption animation CSS/React =E2=80=94 hundreds of lines plu=
s
edge-case testing.

Integration: LOW.

Maintenance risk: LOW-MEDIUM; coupled to Remotion APIs.

Exact experiment: feed HeadTTS word timestamps for 50 roast lines into
createCaptionPages() with a Roast.pet KineticSlam theme.
Screenshot-test line breaks and safe-area placement at 9:16, 1:1 and
16:9. Compare against our current caption implementation.

Verdict: REUSE NOW if Remotion remains; otherwise copy the pagination
rules/mechanism.

4) dh-js/printify-etsy-listing-creation-desktop-app =E2=80=94 COPY
FAILURE/RESUME MECHANISM, WATCH LICENSE

URL: https://github.com/dh-js/printify-etsy-listing-creation-desktop-app

Creator: dh-js

Activity: 21 commits; 4 stars / 1 fork

License: no clear license surfaced; do not copy source until verified.

What it solves: This is an actual client-built Printify=E2=86=92Etsy
production automation, not a toy wrapper. A CSV + files directory
drives Printify product/listing creation; it then finds the published
Etsy listing and uploads custom images AND listing video as binary
through Etsy. Crucially, it writes frequent progress backups to CSV
and can resume incomplete runs without repeating completed work. It
supports per-product variants/colors/sizes/pricing and continues past
non-critical errors.

Install/route: Node desktop/server app packaged to a single executable
with PKG; Etsy OAuth2 + Printify/Etsy APIs. It uses an ngrok URL for
Printify assets over 5 MB.

Dependencies/hardware: Node, API credentials, network.

Evidence: 21-commit client project; explicit resume-from-backup
workflow and custom Etsy image/video post-processing after Printify
publish.

Work replaced: concrete reference for the exact Roast.pet handoff from
Printify to Etsy media patching and crash recovery.

Integration: MEDIUM.

Maintenance risk: HIGH as literal code because API assumptions may age
and license is unclear.

Exact experiment: do not copy code. Reproduce its state machine with
one disposable Printify/Etsy draft: create product =E2=86=92 publish =E2=86=
=92 locate
Etsy listing =E2=86=92 upload Roast.pet listing images/video =E2=86=92 cras=
h
intentionally =E2=86=92 resume from checkpoint without duplicating
product/listing.

Verdict: COPY MECHANISM / WATCH LICENSE.

TOP 5 THINGS WE SHOULD NOT BUILD OURSELVES

- An arbitrary-audio lip-sync correction workstation:
lip-sync-generator already has exposure-sheet editing, mouth
placement, keyframes and exports.

- A browser media compositor/encoder before benchmarking YumCut;
WebCodecs + WebGL2 + muxing/support analysis are already packaged.

- Short-form caption pagination and animation edge cases if using
Remotion; remotion-captions-kit already handles
sentence/silence/orphan/abbreviation logic.

- Etsy listing-video patching and crash-resume logic from first
principles; existing production scripts demonstrate the sequence.

- Whole-order restart behavior. Persist checkpoints and resume only
the incomplete Printify/Etsy/media stage.

Most surprising hobby project: lip-sync-generator. A single offline
HTML file has effectively become a miniature Adobe Character Animator
mouth workflow: DSP + Rhubarb, editable exposure sheet, keyframed
mouth placement, puppet compositing, 24-clip queue and eight export
modes.

Fastest integration win: remotion-captions-kit. HeadTTS already gives
Roast.pet word timings; feeding those directly into this kit could
eliminate our caption pagination/timing edge cases immediately.

Repo/author worth following: Doug Alder / lip-sync-generator. The
project is solving exactly the unglamorous human-correction and export
details that make automated character speech usable in production,
rather than merely demonstrating a model.

Architecture implication: the browser can plausibly own substantially
more of Roast.pet than expected: customer cleanup
(Alphaveil/BiRefNet), arbitrary-audio mouth correction
(lip-sync-generator), caption logic (remotion-captions-kit), and
possibly complete interactive rendering (YumCut). Server-side
infrastructure can then concentrate on generation, identity QA,
durable order state and commerce rather than media plumbing.
